We classify each element into three categories:
- 0: less than `a`
- 1: in `[a, b]`
- 2: greater than `b`

A "good" array is one where all 0s come first, then all 1s, then all 2s (like sorting by category).

The minimum number of adjacent swaps to transform one binary/multi-class sequence into a sorted sequence: Since swaps are adjacent and elements within the same class are indistinguishable, the minimum adjacent swaps equals... well, for two categories, it's counting inversions-like quantity. For three categories, the minimum adjacent swaps to sort a sequence by category (stable sort essentially, but since elements within a class are indistinguishable, we just need to match positions).

Actually, the minimum number of adjacent swaps to transform sequence into target arrangement where equal elements are indistinguishable: we can greedily match the k-th occurrence of each class in the original to the k-th occurrence in the target, and count the number of inversions between matched positions. But when target is just sorted classes, there's a simpler formula.

The minimum adjacent swaps to sort by class (0s first, then 1s, then 2s) equals the number of pairs (i, j) with i < j but class(i) > class(j) — wait, that's the number of inversions of the class sequence. Since each adjacent swap changes inversion count by exactly 1, and the sorted sequence has 0 inversions, the minimum number of swaps equals the inversion count of the class sequence!

Wait, but careful: swapping two adjacent elements of the same class is useless. Minimum adjacent swaps to reach sorted = inversion count where inversions count pairs with class(i) > class(j). Yes, that's standard: adjacent swap sorting requires exactly inversion count swaps, and since classes equal elements are indistinguishable, inversion count defined by strict greater class is right.

So answer = number of pairs i < j with class(i) > class(j), modulo 1e9+7.

