Assistant: All three examples pass. The key insight: a "good" array is exactly the class sequence (0 = <a, 1 = in [a,b], 2 = >b) sorted in ascending order, and the minimum number of adjacent swaps to sort a sequence equals its inversion count. This gives an O(n) single-pass solution.

```python
class Solution:
    def minAdjacentSwaps(self, nums: list[int], a: int, b: int) -> int:
        MOD = 10**9 + 7
        # Classify: 0 -> < a, 1 -> in [a, b], 2 -> > b
        # A good array is exactly the class sequence sorted ascending.
        # Minimum adjacent swaps = number of inversions in the class sequence.
        cnt1 = 0  # number of class-1 elements seen so far
        cnt2 = 0  # number of class-2 elements seen so far
        inv = 0
        for x in nums:
            if x < a:
                # class 0: every earlier class-1 or class-2 element forms an inversion
                inv += cnt1 + cnt2
            elif x <= b:
                # class 1: every earlier class-2 element forms an inversion
                inv += cnt2
                cnt1 += 1
            else:
                cnt2 += 1
        return inv % MOD
```
